given on BritBase was} 45... Kg7 {, which I sourced from Roger Paige's 2005
book 'The Chess Games of Sir George Thomas'. Had Black played 45...Kg7, he
would have been liable to lose as follows:} 46. h5 $1 gxh5 47. Kxf5 Kf7 48. g6+
Kg7 49. Kg5 h4 50. Kxh4 Kxg6 51. Kg4 Kf6 52. Kf4 Ke7 53. Ke5 Kd7 54. d6 cxd6+
55. Kd5 {, etc. Paige gives the Field as his source for the game - I do not
know whether the Field gave the move incorrectly or whether this was a later
keying error.}) 46. Kf4 (46. h5 gxh5 47. Kxf5 Kg7 $1 $11) 46... Kf7 47. Ke5 {
Sources: The Field 20 Sept 1923, p430 (via the 2005 book 'The Chess Games of
Sir George Thomas' by Roger Leslie Paige); (42b-end) BCM, September 1923, p316}
