1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c3 Bf5 5. Ne2 e6 6. Ng3 Bg6 7. Nd2 Nf6 8. Be2
Nc6 9. Bf3 Rc8 10. Qe2 Bd6 11. Ndf1 Qc7 12. Bg5 O-O 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. Ne3 Kg7
15. O-O Rh8 16. Rfe1 h5 17. Ngf1 Rcg8 18. Qd1 Rh6 19. Rc1 Qb8 20. c4 Bb4 21.
cxd5 exd5 22. Re2 Be4 23. Bxe4 dxe4 24. Nf5+ {"Some amusement arose at the
Chess Congress at Southsea on Wednesday from an extraordinary incident in the
game between Jacobs and Gooding. Jacobs checked with a Kt forking King and
Rook, and the astonishing thing was that neither player noticed the check.
Jacobs had not said "Check," and Gooding went on with the line of play that he
had previously intended, while Jacobs left the board, and on returning
captured the Rook. A few more moves were made, when, on official intervention,
the rule was enforced requiring the players to go back to the position in
which the illegality occurred, and Jacobs soon after­wards won." (Sevenoaks
Chronicle and Kentish Advertiser - Friday 24 August 1923) BCM gives the
illegal continuation: 25...Qf4 26.Nxh6 Kxh6 26.Ng3 at which point a
representative of the tournament committee stepped in and reported the
illegality.} Kh7 25. Nxh6 Kxh6 26. Rxe4 {Source of game moves: Tinsley
notebooks} 1-0

